# {{ project.name }}

A fast command-line tool written in Rust, shipped as a single binary: `{{ env.APP_SLUG }}`.

## Requirements

{% if choices.docker == "full" -%}
- Docker 24 or newer with Docker Compose. Everything runs in containers; Rust is not needed on this machine.
{%- else -%}
- Rust 1.85 or newer from [rustup](https://rustup.rs), with `rustfmt` and `clippy` (included in the default profile; otherwise `rustup component add rustfmt clippy`)
{%- if choices.docker == "run" %}
- Docker 24 or newer with Docker Compose (only for the Docker image)
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}

## Quick start

```sh
[ -f .env ] || cp .env.example .env   # first run after cloning
{%- if choices.docker == "full" %}
docker compose run --rm dev cargo fetch
docker compose up dev                             # development loop (see below)
docker compose run --rm --build app --help        # the production image
docker compose run --rm app stats README.md Cargo.toml
{%- else %}
cargo fetch
sh scripts/dev.sh                    # development loop (see below)
cargo run -- --help
cargo run -- stats README.md Cargo.toml
echo "hello world" | cargo run -q -- stats
{%- endif %}
```

The development loop builds the tool and runs it with `DEV_ARGS` from `.env` (`stats README.md Cargo.toml`), then again whenever a file in `src/`, `build.rs`, `Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock` or `.env` changes. Pass other arguments to override them: `sh scripts/dev.sh stats --format json Cargo.toml`.

## Usage

```text
{{ env.APP_SLUG }} [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  stats  Count lines, words, characters and bytes of files or standard input
  help   Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -v, --verbose...  Show more log output on stderr (-v info, -vv debug, -vvv trace)
  -q, --quiet       Show only errors on stderr
  -h, --help        Print help
  -V, --version     Print version
```

Examples:

```sh
{{ env.APP_SLUG }} stats notes.txt src/main.rs           # table with a total row
{{ env.APP_SLUG }} stats --format json notes.txt         # JSON for scripts, e.g. | jq .total.words
cat notes.txt | {{ env.APP_SLUG }} stats                 # standard input (also: `-` as a file name)
{{ env.APP_SLUG }} -vv stats notes.txt                   # debug logs on stderr; RUST_LOG=debug works too
```

Results go to stdout, logs and errors to stderr. Exit codes: `0` success, `1` error (e.g. a file cannot be read), `2` invalid arguments.

## Build and install
{% if choices.docker == "full" %}
Builds happen in Docker and produce Linux binaries, so the tool is used through its image:

```sh
docker compose build app                          # image {{ project.slug }}:latest
docker compose run --rm --build app stats README.md
docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/work" {{ project.slug }} stats README.md   # the same without Compose
```
{% else %}
```sh
cargo build --release                # target/release/{{ env.APP_SLUG }}, a single self-contained binary
cargo install --path . --locked      # installs {{ env.APP_SLUG }} into ~/.cargo/bin (on your PATH with rustup)
```

The binary runs on machines with the same operating system and CPU architecture; copy it anywhere.
{% endif %}
## Development
{% if choices.docker == "full" %}
Run commands in the dev container: `docker compose run --rm dev <command>` (`sh scripts/dev.sh` → `docker compose up dev`).
{% endif %}
| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `sh scripts/dev.sh [args…]` | Build and run on every change (default arguments: `DEV_ARGS` in `.env`) |
| `cargo run -- <args>` | Build and run once |
| `cargo build --release` | Optimised binary in `target/release/` |
| `cargo test` | Unit tests (in `src/`) and black-box tests of the binary (in `tests/`) |
| `sh scripts/check.sh` | Format check, clippy (warnings are errors) and tests — the quality gate before commits |
| `cargo fmt --all` | Format all files |
| `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` | Lints |

## Configuration

`.env` in the project root configures the build and the development loop; it is git-ignored, and `.env.example` documents every key. The binary itself does not read `.env` at run time: its behaviour is controlled by options and by `RUST_LOG` (log filter) and `NO_COLOR`.

| Key | Value | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `APP_NAME` | `{{ env.APP_NAME }}` | Display name, compiled into the binary by `build.rs` (first line of `--help`) |
| `APP_SLUG` | `{{ env.APP_SLUG }}` | Executable name (`package.name` in `Cargo.toml`) |
| `DEV_ARGS` | `stats README.md Cargo.toml` | Arguments of the development loop |
{%- if choices.docker != "none" %}
| `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` | `{{ project.slug }}` | Docker Compose project and image name |
{%- endif %}

## Project structure

```text
src/main.rs        Entry point: arguments, logging, exit codes
src/cli.rs         Command-line interface (clap): global options and commands
src/commands/      One module per command: arguments, input/output, rendering
src/domain/        The logic behind the commands, free of I/O and command-line concerns
src/output.rs      Text and JSON output
tests/             Black-box tests that run the compiled binary
scripts/           Development loop and quality gate
build.rs           Compiles APP_NAME from .env into the binary
```

The example command **stats** shows every layer end to end: `cli.rs` → `commands/stats.rs` → `domain/stats.rs` → text or JSON output, with unit and black-box tests.
